column90:
The reason why `this` is set to the window is because of the way the `this` keyword is bound in JavaScript. It is bound at call time.<p>If at call time it is being called on an object, it will have the value of that object. Otherwise it will have the value of window.<p><pre><code>    var obj = { f: function () { return this; } };

    // returns the object
    obj.f()

    // returns window
    var f = obj.f;
    f();
</code></pre>
What is happening here is that the setTimeout() calls the lambda at some later point, and it's no longer in the context of the object.<p>An alternate fix for the issue would be:<p><pre><code>    var obj = (function () {
        var that = {};

        that.doSomethingLater = function () {
            setTimeout(function () { console.log(that); }, 1000);
        };

        return that;
    })();

    obj.doSomethingLater();
</code></pre>
An article on the subject (that also talks about call and apply): <a href="http://www.robertsosinski.com/2009/04/28/binding-scope-in-javascript/" rel="nofollow">http://www.robertsosinski.com/2009/04/28/binding-scope-in-ja...</a>
